Body:
I've been playing around with a replacement edit tool that doesn't need to generate the oldString at all. Instead it uses system calls to read the lines of the file into memory determinstically (so it doesn't need to generate the oldString) and uses it directly. It only has to generate the replacement string.

This tool is a little faster than the old edit tool since it doesn't do 2 generate steps (old and new), it only generates once (the new).

Put it in your opencode folder here, when you launch opencode it will pick up the edit tool and replace its internal default edit tool with this one:

```
~/.config/opencode/tools/edit.ts
```

Here's the tool:

```
import { tool } from "@opencode-ai/plugin"
import * as fs from "fs/promises"

// Replaces this file's built-in `edit` tool (same name = override).
//
// The built-in tool matches an exact `oldString` snippet and requires
// repeating unchanged surrounding lines in `newString`. Small/local models
// constantly get that snippet slightly wrong (whitespace, picking a snippet
// that isn't unique, typos when retranscribing context) and burn a retry
// every time. Addressing by line number instead - just startLine/endLine
// plus the new content - avoids both problems, since the model never has
// to transcribe lines it isn't changing.

// Builds a single-hunk unified diff (3 lines of context) for the edited
// range, in the format the opencode TUI expects for its diff view.
function unifiedDiff(
  filePath: string,
  oldLines: string[],
  startLine: number,
  endLine: number,
  replacement: string[],
): string {
  const CONTEXT = 3
  const removedCount = Math.max(0, endLine - startLine + 1)

  const contextBefore = Math.min(CONTEXT, startLine - 1)
  const contextAfter = Math.min(CONTEXT, oldLines.length - endLine)

  const oldStart = startLine - contextBefore
  const oldCount = contextBefore + removedCount + contextAfter
  const newStart = oldStart
  const newCount = contextBefore + replacement.length + contextAfter

  const hunkLines: string[] = []
  for (let i = oldStart; i < startLine; i++) hunkLines.push(` ${oldLines[i - 1]}`)
  for (let i = startLine; i <= endLine; i++) hunkLines.push(`-${oldLines[i - 1]}`)
  for (const line of replacement) hunkLines.push(`+${line}`)
  for (let i = endLine + 1; i <= endLine + contextAfter; i++) hunkLines.push(` ${oldLines[i - 1]}`)

  return (
    `--- ${filePath}\n` +
    `+++ ${filePath}\n` +
    `@@ -${oldStart},${oldCount} +${newStart},${newCount} @@\n` +
    hunkLines.join("\n") +
    "\n"
  )
}

export default tool({
  description:
    "Edit a file by line number. Use the read or grep tool first to find the correct " +
    "line numbers - the read tool returns lines as '<line>: <content>'. " +
    "mode='replace' (default) replaces lines startLine..endLine (1-indexed, inclusive) " +
    "with newContent - omit endLine to replace just startLine. " +
    "If newContent has more lines than the range being replaced, set endLine to cover " +
    "every line you're touching - any extra newContent lines beyond that range are " +
    "inserted, NOT used to overwrite the lines that follow. " +
    "mode='insertBefore'/'insertAfter' insert newContent as new lines before/after " +
    "startLine without removing anything - endLine is ignored for these modes. " +
    "To delete lines, use mode='replace' with an empty newContent. " +
    "IMPORTANT: newContent must contain ONLY the actual file content - do NOT include " +
    "the '<line>: ' prefix shown by the read tool, and do NOT add a trailing newline " +
    "or '\\n' at the end - the tool handles line breaks between lines automatically.",
  args: {
